Write a C++ function named `insertOrAppend` that takes a `std::forward_list<int>`, two integers `target` and `value`, and an optional `capacity` parameter representing the maximum number of elements the forward list can hold. The function should insert `value` after the first occurrence of `target` in the forward list, ensuring all elements are unique within the specified capacity. If `target` is not found, `value` should be appended to the end of the list, provided it does not exceed the `capacity`. If adding `value` would exceed the `capacity`, the function should truncate the list to maintain the specified limit.

**Design Challenges**:
- Efficiently locate and insert `value` after `target` while handling large lists.
- Managing list truncation when the `capacity` constraint is violated.

**Engineering Tradeoffs**:
- Balancing memory usage with performance for large datasets.
- Choosing between hash-based and linear search approaches for maintaining uniqueness, considering both time complexity and space efficiency.

**Demonstrative Examples**:
- Safe Code Example:
  ```cpp
  void safeInsertOrAppend(std::forward_list<int>& list, int target, int value, size_t capacity) {
      auto it = list.begin();
      bool found = false;
      for (; it != list.end() && !found; ++it) {
          if (*it == target) {
              found = true;
          }
      }
      if (!found) {
          if (list.size() < capacity) {
              list.push_back(value);
          } else {
              // Implement logic to truncate the list to maintain capacity
          }
      } else {
          // Insert value after the found target
      }
  }
  ```

- Unsafe Code Example:
  ```cpp
  void unsafeInsertOrAppend(std::forward_list<int>& list, int target, int value, size_t capacity) {
      if (list.size() >= capacity) {
          // Truncate logic here
      }
      auto it = list.begin();
      for (; it != list.end(); ++it) {
          if (*it == target) {
              *it = value; // Overwrites the target value instead of inserting after
              return;
          }
      }
      list.push_back(value); // May exceed capacity without check
  }
  ```

mutator:
['```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <unordered_set>

void insertOrAppend(std::forward_list<int>& list, int target, int value, size_t capacity = std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max()) {
    auto it = list.begin();
    bool found = false;
    std::unordered_set<int> uniqueElements;

    // Ensure the current elements are in the set for uniqueness check
    for (const auto& elem : list) {
        uniqueElements.insert(elem);
    }

    while (it != list.end() && !found) {
        if (*it == target) {
            found = true;
        }
        ++it;
    }

    // Check for uniqueness and capacity before insertion or appending
    if (uniqueElements.find(value) == uniqueElements.end()) {
        if (list.size() < capacity) {
            if (found) {
                list.insert_after(it, value);
            } else {
                list.push_front(value);  // Insert at front to maintain order when target not found
            }

            uniqueElements.insert(value);

            // Truncate the list if it exceeds the capacity
            if (list.size() > capacity) {
                auto newEnd = std::next(list.begin(), static_cast<ptrdiff_t>(capacity));
                list.erase_after(newEnd);
            }
        }
    } else if (!found && list.size() < capacity) {
        // Append only if unique and not found, and within capacity
        list.push_front(value);  // Insert at front to maintain order when target not found

        // Truncate the list if it exceeds the capacity after appending
        if (list.size() > capacity) {
            auto newEnd = std::next(list.begin(), static_cast<ptrdiff_t>(capacity));
            list.erase_after(newEnd);
        }
    }
}
